// ============================================================================
//  SECTION 14 : SETUP STATE MACHINE  --  THE SPINE
//
//  Every detector above this line answers ONE question about the current bar.
//  This section is the only thing in the file that remembers a TRADE IDEA
//  across bars: it births a Setup when SECTION 5 reports a sweep, walks it
//  forward through the SetupState ladder as each later detector confirms, and
//  emits exactly one signal per direction per bar when the chain completes.
//
//  THE TWO INVARIANTS THAT MAKE THIS NON-REPAINTING
//    (1) RANK STRICTLY INCREASES. f_smEnter() refuses any transition whose
//        target rank is not strictly greater than the current rank. There is
//        no backward edge and no self-edge, so a state, once stamped with its
//        stBar, can never be revisited, re-stamped or downgraded. A label
//        anchored to a historical transition cannot move because the
//        transition itself cannot be un-made.
//    (2) `dead` IS ABSORBING. f_smStep() only looks at ranks 0..6 (idle..armed)
//        and `dead` is rank 8, so nothing can leave it.
//
//  The ARCHITECT RELAXED the draft's stricter "advancement is always rank+1"
//  rule to plain "rank strictly increases", for one concrete reason: SECTION 10
//  offers a Confirmation entry mode that has no retest leg, and under rank+1
//  the bos -> armed edge it needs was ILLEGAL. Mode 1 was unreachable code.
//  Strict monotonicity preserves the whole non-repaint argument and legalises
//  exactly the skips the entry modes require. Both legal skip edges are
//  bos -> armed and bos -> retest -> armed, and which one fires is decided by
//  SECTION 10's trigger, not by a mode string duplicated here.
//
//  THE ARMED STATE HAS A REAL ONE-BAR DEADLINE. In the draft the step window
//  stopped at `retest`, so `armed` was never evaluated and its documented
//  timeout was dead code: a setup that armed but was not the one promoted sat
//  in `armed` forever, was never invalidated, never timed out, never evicted
//  (the eviction filter protects everything above rank 0) and was still counted
//  as live - so the direction ran out of slots and silently went dark for the
//  rest of the chart. Here `armed` is inside the step window, the deadline is
//  enforced, and step 14b additionally reaps every armed setup that was not
//  promoted on the bar it armed. No setup can survive its own arming bar
//  unpromoted.
//
//  ONE ARRAY, NOT TWO. The per-direction cap is the invariant that matters and
//  it is enforced on a dir-filtered LIVE count; a second array would only give
//  SECTION 16 two things to draw and two places to get the index wrong.
//
//  TWO-PHASE DEATH, WITH NO EXCEPTIONS. kill() marks dead, stamps the reason
//  and stamps stBar - it never frees a handle. The NEXT bar's step 1 calls
//  .wipe() and removes the element. Eviction goes through the SAME path (the
//  draft freed and removed an evicted victim inline, which is a second death
//  path and the one place a handle can be freed twice or a death can vanish
//  before SECTION 15 sees it). Therefore: exactly one call site for .wipe(),
//  exactly one code path, every death visible to SECTION 15 on its death bar.
//
//  WHAT THIS SECTION DOES NOT DO, on purpose:
//    * NO detection. It never asks whether a sweep happened; it reads sweepSigL.
//    * NO risk maths. f_riskPlan() belongs to SECTION 11 and is called here
//      EXACTLY ONCE per setup, on the bar it arms, with entryPx = close.
//    * NO gating. gateL / gateS are SECTION 13's first-match-wins verdict and
//      this section treats them as the single authority.
//    * NO zone lifecycle. SECTION 6 owns array<IcebergZone>; a Setup carries
//      the zone ID as identity plus a frozen snapshot of the bounds so its
//      drawing and its stop cannot move after the fact.
// ============================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- S14 GROUPS
G14L = "14 - Setup Lifecycle"
G14T = "14 - Setup Lifecycle Timeouts"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- S14 INPUTS
i_maxSetupsPerDir = input.int(2, "Max live setups per direction", minval = 1,
     maxval = 4, group = G14L,
     tooltip = "Hard cap on concurrent UNFINISHED setups per direction. At 1 " +
               "the engine commits to the first candidate and ignores a better " +
               "one that forms behind it; at 4 four half-formed candidates " +
               "compete for the same confirmations and the chart fills with " +
               "boxes that never arm. Open trades do NOT consume a slot.",
     display = display.none)

i_maxHopsPerBar = input.int(2, "Max state hops per bar", minval = 1, maxval = 3,
     group = G14L,
     tooltip = "How many forward states one setup may advance inside a single " +
               "confirmed bar. 1 is strictest - one state per candle - and " +
               "will miss the single decisive candle that IS both the sweep " +
               "and the absorption, which is how a real gold trap prints. At 3 " +
               "a very clean candle can carry a setup from birth to armed in " +
               "one bar; nothing repaints, but the six confirmations are then " +
               "six facts about one candle rather than six about six.",
     display = display.none)

i_conflictMode = input.string("Best score wins", "Long vs short on one bar",
     options = ["Best score wins", "Kill both"], group = G14L,
     tooltip = "What to do when a long and a short arm on the SAME bar. An " +
               "EXACT score tie always resolves to NO TRADE under either " +
               "setting: equal scores mean the model has no opinion. 'Allow " +
               "both' is deliberately not offered - a TradingView strategy " +
               "holds one net position, so the second entry would reverse the " +
               "first rather than hedge it, and the indicator would then " +
               "disagree with its own strategy twin.",
     display = display.none)

i_killOnSessionEnd = input.bool(true, "Kill everything at the day rollover",
     group = G14L,
     tooltip = "Gold's structure does not survive the settlement gap and the " +
               "thin rollover hour. ON discards every unfinished setup AND " +
               "flattens every open idea at the new trading day (the day " +
               "boundary is the one set by the timezone input in SECTION 0). " +
               "OFF carries swept levels and absorption zones across the gap, " +
               "which is how a setup ends up anchored to a price that no " +
               "longer exists.",
     display = display.none)

i_barsToAbsorb = input.int(4, "TRAP: bars from sweep to rejection", minval = 1,
     maxval = 50, group = G14T,
     tooltip = "Deadline on the `idle` and `absorbing` states. If absorption " +
               "has not shown itself this fast, nothing was being absorbed - " +
               "the sweep was just a move. Too low and every setup dies in the " +
               "trap phase; too high and the engine holds stale ideas through " +
               "a trend leg that already invalidated them.",
     display = display.none)

i_barsToBreak = input.int(8, "BREAK: bars from rejection to BOS", minval = 1,
     maxval = 100, group = G14T,
     tooltip = "Deadline on `rejected` and `displaced`. Real absorption is " +
               "followed by displacement quickly; a slow grind away from the " +
               "zone is drift, not a reversal, and this is what expires it.",
     display = display.none)

i_barsToRetest = input.int(15, "ENTRY: bars from BOS to armed", minval = 1,
     maxval = 200, group = G14T,
     tooltip = "Deadline on `bos` and `retest` - the most generous window, " +
               "because the pullback is the part that legitimately takes time. " +
               "Set it very high and the engine will chase a displacement that " +
               "has already decayed; set it very low and only V-shaped " +
               "retests ever arm.",
     display = display.none)

i_barsInTrade = input.int(60, "TRADE: max bars in trade (time stop)", minval = 5,
     maxval = 500, group = G14T,
     tooltip = "THE time stop, and the only one in the file - SECTION 15's " +
               "virtual ledger reads this same input so the setup and its " +
               "trade record can never expire on different bars. An idea that " +
               "has neither reached target nor been stopped in this many bars " +
               "has lost its edge and is booked flat at the close.",
     display = display.none)

i_invalidAtrMult = input.float(0.35, "Hard-invalidation buffer (x ATR)",
     minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = G14L,
     tooltip = "Passed to f_tol(), so the buffer is this many ATR but never " +
               "thinner than one spread. A CLOSE this far the wrong side of " +
               "the sweep extreme, the zone or the displacement origin kills " +
               "the setup. At 0 the buffer collapses to one spread and normal " +
               "quote breathing will kill valid setups; above ~1.0 nothing is " +
               "ever invalidated and every setup dies of timeout instead, " +
               "which throws away the most informative failure signal there is.",
     display = display.none)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.1  THE INTRABAR PATH POLICY - declared here, obeyed everywhere
//
//  Bar-resolution data genuinely cannot tell whether the stop or the target was
//  touched first inside a bar that contains both. This file ALWAYS assumes the
//  STOP FILLED FIRST. That is deliberately pessimistic, it can only make the
//  reported result worse than reality, and it is what keeps this ledger honest
//  next to TradingView's broker emulator, which guesses the path from the bar's
//  open and will therefore look better than this on exactly the bars that
//  matter most. SECTION 15 reuses these two primitives rather than restating
//  the rule, so there is one definition of "touched" in the file.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_pathStop(int dir, float px) => na(px) ? false : dir > 0 ? low  <= px : high >= px
f_pathTgt (int dir, float px) => na(px) ? false : dir > 0 ? high >= px : low  <= px

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.2  RANK AND DEADLINE
//  f_smRankOf() is the SAME ladder as Setup.rank() in SECTION 2, taking a bare
//  SetupState instead of a Setup, because f_smEnter() has to rank a state it
//  has not committed yet. If one is ever edited the other must follow.
//  Deadlines are PER STATE, measured from stBar. The frozen Setup type carries
//  stBar and no phaseBar, so a per-PHASE clock is not expressible; per state is
//  marginally more generous (a rejected -> displaced hop restarts the BREAK
//  clock) and is stated here rather than quietly assumed.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smRankOf(SetupState st) =>
    switch st
        SetupState.idle      => 0
        SetupState.absorbing => 1
        SetupState.rejected  => 2
        SetupState.displaced => 3
        SetupState.bos       => 4
        SetupState.retest    => 5
        SetupState.armed     => 6
        SetupState.active    => 7
        => 8

f_smDeadline(SetupState st) =>
    switch st
        SetupState.idle      => i_barsToAbsorb
        SetupState.absorbing => i_barsToAbsorb
        SetupState.rejected  => i_barsToBreak
        SetupState.displaced => i_barsToBreak
        SetupState.bos       => i_barsToRetest
        SetupState.retest    => i_barsToRetest
        SetupState.armed     => 1
        SetupState.active    => i_barsInTrade
        => 1000000

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.3  THE COLLECTION + THE ARM TRIGGERS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var array<Setup> setups = array.new<Setup>()

// Hoisted to global scope and evaluated on EVERY bar, unconditionally, so that
// nothing inside the state machine's lazily-evaluated conditions can skip them.
// gateL / gateS are SECTION 13's first-match-wins verdict and carry the score
// test, the R:R test, the cooldowns and the duplicate test, so they are the one
// authority here; coreOk is SECTION 12's "the evidence chain actually closed"
// flag and is required as well, because a contender that cannot show core
// evidence must not be allowed to arm and then veto its mirror.
s14ArmL = entryRawL and coreOkL and gateL == NoTradeReason.ok
s14ArmS = entryRawS and coreOkS and gateS == NoTradeReason.ok

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.4  LIFECYCLE PRIMITIVES
//  kill() NEVER frees a drawing. There is exactly one .wipe() call site in this
//  file and it is in the garbage collector at step 1.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smKill(Setup s, NoTradeReason why) =>
    s.st    := SetupState.dead
    s.stBar := bar_index
    s.death := why
    true

f_smEnter(Setup s, SetupState nxt) =>
    // INVARIANT (1), enforced in code rather than asserted in prose.
    bool ok = f_smRankOf(nxt) > s.rank()
    if ok
        s.st    := nxt
        s.stBar := bar_index
    ok

f_smLive(int dir) =>
    // An ACTIVE setup is a position, not a candidate, so it does not consume a
    // candidate slot. Counting it would let one open trade block every new
    // birth in its direction for i_barsInTrade bars - which on 15M is the best
    // part of a session - for no reason connected to the quality of the idea.
    int c = 0
    int n = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            int r = s.rank()
            if s.dir == dir and r <= 6
                c += 1
    c

f_smHasActive(int dir) =>
    bool found = false
    int n = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            if s.dir == dir and s.st == SetupState.active
                found := true
    found

// Weakest EVICTABLE peer: rank 0 (`idle`) only, oldest first. THE eviction
// policy in one line - a newborn may only ever displace another newborn.
// Anything that has reached `absorbing` has shown evidence and is protected.
f_smWeakestIdx(int dir) =>
    int wIdx = -1
    int wBar = 2147483647
    int n    = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            if s.dir == dir and s.st == SetupState.idle and s.bornBar < wBar
                wIdx := i
                wBar := s.bornBar
    wIdx

f_smMakeRoom(int dir) =>
    bool ok = f_smLive(dir) < i_maxSetupsPerDir
    if not ok
        int wi = f_smWeakestIdx(dir)
        if wi >= 0
            f_smKill(setups.get(wi), NoTradeReason.dEvicted)
            ok := true
    ok

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.5  HARD INVALIDATION - returns the reason that ACTUALLY fired
//  Returning a NoTradeReason rather than a bool is what lets SECTION 15's
//  histogram say WHICH thesis broke instead of which stage it broke in. All
//  three tests use CLOSE, never a wick: a wick through the level is the trap
//  this system trades, so killing on it would delete the setup at the exact
//  moment it becomes valid. Every test is a true mirror - the only difference
//  between long and short is the sign of dir.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smInvalid(Setup s) =>
    bool  up  = s.dir > 0
    float buf = f_tol(i_invalidAtrMult)
    int   r   = s.rank()
    NoTradeReason why = NoTradeReason.ok

    // (1) The sweep was genuine continuation, not a trap: there was nothing
    //     resting there to absorb.
    if not na(s.swpExt)
        bool gone = up ? close < s.swpExt - buf : close > s.swpExt + buf
        if gone
            why := NoTradeReason.dFalseSweep

    // (2) Price closed back through the absorption zone. Only meaningful once
    //     the rejection has printed - before that the zone is still forming.
    if why == NoTradeReason.ok and r >= 2 and not na(s.zoneTop) and not na(s.zoneBot)
        bool lost = up ? close < s.zoneBot - buf : close > s.zoneTop + buf
        if lost
            why := NoTradeReason.dZoneBroken

    // (3) SECTION 6 itself declared the zone dead. Only applied when the zone
    //     the setup was born on is still the CURRENT one for its side - a
    //     superseded zone is not an invalidated one.
    if why == NoTradeReason.ok and r >= 2 and s.zoneId != 0
        int  zid   = up ? zoneIdL    : zoneIdS
        bool alive = up ? zoneAliveL : zoneAliveS
        if s.zoneId == zid and not alive
            why := NoTradeReason.dZoneBroken

    // (4) The displacement leg has been fully retraced after the break, so the
    //     break was a fake. dispOrg is the leg's near edge (SECTION 8's
    //     canonical scalar), which is why this is a real test and not a
    //     restatement of (2).
    if why == NoTradeReason.ok and r >= 4 and not na(s.dispOrg)
        bool faded = up ? close < s.dispOrg - buf : close > s.dispOrg + buf
        if faded
            why := NoTradeReason.dBosFail
    why

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.6  GEOMETRY FREEZE
//  Returns NoTradeReason.ok to accept the transition, or the reason to kill on.
//  Everything a Setup will ever be drawn or traded from is copied into the
//  object HERE, at the instant the detector that owns it says so, and is never
//  recomputed. That is the whole of the "historical labels do not move" claim.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smCapture(Setup s, SetupState nxt) =>
    bool up = s.dir > 0
    NoTradeReason why = NoTradeReason.ok
    switch nxt
        SetupState.absorbing =>
            s.qAbsorb := up ? qAbsorbL : qAbsorbS
            s.qRvol   := up ? qRvolL   : qRvolS
            s.zoneId  := up ? zoneIdL  : zoneIdS
            s.zoneTop := up ? zoneTopL : zoneTopS
            s.zoneBot := up ? zoneBotL : zoneBotS
            // Never freeze an na or inverted zone: it would produce a stop on
            // the wrong side of entry and a plausible-looking plan with
            // nonsensical geometry that only ever exits on the time stop.
            bool bad = na(s.zoneTop) or na(s.zoneBot) or s.zoneTop <= s.zoneBot
            if bad
                why := NoTradeReason.zoneDead
            why
        SetupState.rejected =>
            s.qReject := up ? qRejectL : qRejectS
            why
        SetupState.displaced =>
            s.qDisp   := up ? qDispL   : qDispS
            s.dispOrg := up ? dispOrgL : dispOrgS
            s.dispFar := up ? dispFarL : dispFarS
            if na(s.dispOrg)
                why := NoTradeReason.noDisplace
            why
        SetupState.bos =>
            s.qBos    := up ? qBosL      : qBosS
            s.bosLvl  := up ? bosLvlL    : bosLvlS
            s.retestLv := up ? retestLvlL : retestLvlS
            // No "is the level already broken" re-test here: SECTION 9 owns
            // break detection and bosSigL is by construction the bar the close
            // took the level out. Re-deriving it here is how displaced -> bos
            // turns into a rubber stamp that counts the displacement leg as
            // its own confirmation.
            if na(s.bosLvl)
                why := NoTradeReason.noBos
            why
        SetupState.armed =>
            s.score   := up ? scoreL   : scoreS
            s.qRetest := up ? qRetestL : qRetestS
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------
            // THE ONE AND ONLY f_riskPlan() CALL FOR THIS SETUP.
            // entryPx is the CLOSE of this confirmed bar - the bar SECTION 10
            // emitted the trigger on - not a price frozen at the BOS up to
            // i_barsToRetest bars ago. The plan is then frozen into the object
            // and every later read (stop, targets, R:R, the alert payload, the
            // strategy's order) is that same plan. SECTION 12 called
            // f_riskPlan() earlier on this same bar with entryPx = close for a
            // provisional qRr, so the two agree by construction.
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------
            float swpLo = up ? s.swpExt : na
            float swpHi = up ? na       : s.swpExt
            RiskPlan pl = f_riskPlan(s.dir, close, s.zoneBot, s.zoneTop,
                 swpLo, swpHi, lastSwLo, lastSwHi,
                 up ? invAnchorL : invAnchorS)
            s.plan := pl
            if na(pl)
                why := NoTradeReason.poorRr
            else if not pl.ok
                why := pl.why == NoTradeReason.ok ? NoTradeReason.poorRr : pl.why
            why
        => why

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.7  THE TRANSITION SWITCH
//  Pure: no side effects, no ta.* calls. Every detector was precomputed at
//  global scope above, so v6's lazy and/or cannot skip one and desynchronise
//  its own history. Two edges leave `bos` - straight to `armed` when SECTION 10
//  triggers (Confirmation mode, and also a Retest-mode trigger that lands on
//  the touch bar itself) and to `retest` when price merely reaches the frozen
//  retest level. Both increase rank, so both are legal.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smRetestTouch(Setup s) =>
    // f_tol(0.05) and not an input: this edge is DESCRIPTIVE - what arms a
    // setup is SECTION 10's trigger, never this touch - so a second tolerance
    // knob here would only be a way to disagree with SECTION 10.
    float t = f_tol(0.05)
    na(s.retestLv) ? false :
         s.dir > 0 ? low <= s.retestLv + t : high >= s.retestLv - t

f_smNext(Setup s) =>
    bool up  = s.dir > 0
    bool arm = up ? s14ArmL : s14ArmS
    // Hoisted out of the switch arm: a multi-line ternary inside a switch arm is
    // legal but unreadable, and f_smRetestTouch() is pure so evaluating it for
    // every state costs nothing and cannot desynchronise anything.
    SetupState fromBos = arm ? SetupState.armed : f_smRetestTouch(s) ? SetupState.retest : s.st
    switch s.st
        SetupState.idle      => (up ? absSigL  : absSigS)  ? SetupState.absorbing : s.st
        SetupState.absorbing => (up ? rejSigL  : rejSigS)  ? SetupState.rejected  : s.st
        SetupState.rejected  => (up ? dispSigL : dispSigS) ? SetupState.displaced : s.st
        SetupState.displaced => (up ? bosSigL  : bosSigS)  ? SetupState.bos       : s.st
        SetupState.bos       => fromBos
        SetupState.retest    => arm ? SetupState.armed : s.st
        => s.st

// One transition attempt. PRIORITY IS LOAD-BEARING: invalidation outranks
// timeout outranks advancement, so a setup that is both stale and broken always
// records the more informative reason.
f_smStep(Setup s) =>
    bool moved = false
    int  r     = s.rank()
    if r <= 6
        NoTradeReason inv = f_smInvalid(s)
        if inv != NoTradeReason.ok
            f_smKill(s, inv)
            moved := true
        else if bar_index - s.stBar >= f_smDeadline(s.st)
            f_smKill(s, NoTradeReason.dTimeout)
            moved := true
        else
            SetupState nxt = f_smNext(s)
            if nxt != s.st
                NoTradeReason why = f_smCapture(s, nxt)
                if why == NoTradeReason.ok
                    f_smEnter(s, nxt)
                else
                    f_smKill(s, why)
                moved := true
    moved

f_smAdvance(Setup s) =>
    int  hops = 0
    bool go   = true
    while go and hops < i_maxHopsPerBar
        go := f_smStep(s)
        hops += 1
    hops

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.8  COLLECTION PASSES
//  Every loop freezes its bound in a local first. v6 re-evaluates a for header's
//  `to` expression on every iteration, so `for i = 0 to setups.size() - 1` while
//  pushing or removing inside the body silently changes the iteration count.
//  The GC iterates DOWNWARD so a removal never disturbs an index still to be
//  visited, guarded by `if n > 0` because `for i = -1 to 0` on an empty array
//  counts UP and then indexes -1, which v6 reads as "last element".
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_smGc() =>
    int n = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = n - 1 to 0
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            // stBar < bar_index: a setup killed on THIS bar stays one more bar
            // so SECTION 15 can record its death and SECTION 16 can grey it out.
            if s.st == SetupState.dead and s.stBar < bar_index
                s.wipe()
                setups.remove(i)
    setups.size()

f_smFlush(NoTradeReason why) =>
    int n = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            if s.st != SetupState.dead
                f_smKill(s, why)
    true

f_smPass() =>
    int n = setups.size()          // frozen: nothing is added or removed in here
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            f_smAdvance(setups.get(i))
    n

// Best armed setup in a direction, by the score FROZEN on this setup at its own
// arm capture. Ties on score break on the older setup, which is deterministic
// and independent of array order.
f_smBestArmed(int dir) =>
    int   idx  = -1
    float best = -1.0
    int   bBar = 2147483647
    int   n    = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            if s.dir == dir and s.st == SetupState.armed
                float sc = nz(s.score, -1.0)
                if sc > best or (sc == best and s.bornBar < bBar)
                    best := sc
                    bBar := s.bornBar
                    idx  := i
    idx

// No setup may survive the bar it armed on. The one promoted at step 14 is
// already `active` by the time this runs, so this cannot touch it - which is
// why the reaper needs no "keep this index" argument and cannot get it wrong.
f_smReapArmed(int dir, NoTradeReason why) =>
    int n = setups.size()
    if n > 0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            Setup s = setups.get(i)
            if s.dir == dir and s.st == SetupState.armed
                f_smKill(s, why)
    true

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  14.9  EXPORTED SIGNAL SURFACE
//  Recomputed from false / -1 every bar. sigIdxL/S index into `setups` and are
//  valid only on the bar the signal fires; the GC shifts indices on the next
//  bar, which is why nothing may cache them.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
longSignal  = false
shortSignal = false
sigIdxL     = -1
sigIdxS     = -1

// ============================================================================
//  THE CANONICAL PER-BAR ORDER. This ordering IS the non-repainting argument,
//  so it is written out as a list rather than left to be inferred from the
//  file's line order.
//
//   1. GARBAGE COLLECT   free the handles of everything that died on a PREVIOUS
//                        bar and remove it. The only .wipe() call site.
//   1b SESSION FLUSH     day rollover kills every non-dead setup.
//   2. LEVELS            SECTION 4  - liquidity map, VWAP, day/week extremes
//   3. PRESSURE          SECTION 0  - rvol, delta proxy, buy/sell pressure
//   4. SWEEP             SECTION 5  - sweepSigL/S, extremes, quality
//   5. ABSORPTION        SECTION 6  - zones, absSigL/S
//   6. REJECTION         SECTION 7  - rejSigL/S
//   7. DISPLACEMENT      SECTION 8  - dispSigL/S, origin, FVG
//   8. STRUCTURE / BOS   SECTIONS 3 + 9 - swings, bosSigL/S, CHoCH
//   9. RETEST            SECTION 10 - entryRawL/S, retest level, inv anchor
//  10. RISK + FILTERS    SECTIONS 11 + 13 - f_riskPlan(), gateL/S
//  11. SCORING           SECTION 12 - scoreL/S, coreOkL/S, conflictNow
//       Steps 2-11 are PURE MEASUREMENT. None of them reads setup state, and
//       every ta.* call in the file lives in them, unconditionally, at global
//       scope. That is what makes step 12 free to be as branchy as it likes.
//  12. STATE MACHINE     12a births, then 12b advance. The ONLY writer of
//                        setup state in the entire file.
//  13. CONFLICT          best armed long vs best armed short. LOSERS DIE HERE,
//                        strictly before anything is emitted.
//  14. SIGNAL            promote the survivors to `active` and publish.
//  14b REAP              every armed setup not promoted this bar dies now.
//  15. MANAGEMENT        stop / target / time stop on `active` setups, never on
//                        the bar that set them active.
//  16. DRAW              SECTION 16, display only, outside this gate, and it
//                        never creates, advances, kills or frees anything.
// ============================================================================
if confirmed and warmupOk
    // ---- 1. GARBAGE COLLECT -------------------------------------------------
    f_smGc()

    // ---- 1b. SESSION FLUSH --------------------------------------------------
    if i_killOnSessionEnd and newDay
        f_smFlush(NoTradeReason.dSessionEnd)

    // ---- 12a. BIRTHS --------------------------------------------------------
    // setupSeq is incremented HERE and not inside a helper: a Pine function may
    // not reassign a global, and a one-element array counter would be a second
    // id source to get out of step with SECTION 2B's.
    if sweepSigL
        if f_smMakeRoom(1)
            setupSeq := setupSeq + 1
            setups.push(Setup.new(id = setupSeq, dir = 1, st = SetupState.idle,
                 bornBar = bar_index, stBar = bar_index,
                 swpExt = sweepExtL, qSweep = qSweepL, qLevel = qLevelL))
    if sweepSigS
        if f_smMakeRoom(-1)
            setupSeq := setupSeq + 1
            setups.push(Setup.new(id = setupSeq, dir = -1, st = SetupState.idle,
                 bornBar = bar_index, stBar = bar_index,
                 swpExt = sweepExtS, qSweep = qSweepS, qLevel = qLevelS))

    // ---- 12b. ADVANCE -------------------------------------------------------
    f_smPass()

    // ---- 13. CONFLICT -------------------------------------------------------
    int iL = f_smBestArmed(1)
    int iS = f_smBestArmed(-1)
    if iL >= 0 and iS >= 0
        Setup sl = setups.get(iL)
        Setup ss = setups.get(iS)
        float cL = nz(sl.score, -1.0)
        float cS = nz(ss.score, -1.0)
        // An exact tie means the model has no opinion. NO TRADE, under EVERY
        // policy - the draft buried this inside one branch, so the promise the
        // tooltip makes was false for the other.
        bool tie = cL == cS
        if tie or i_conflictMode == "Kill both"
            f_smKill(sl, NoTradeReason.dConflict)
            f_smKill(ss, NoTradeReason.dConflict)
            iL := -1
            iS := -1
        else if cL > cS
            f_smKill(ss, NoTradeReason.dConflict)
            iS := -1
        else
            f_smKill(sl, NoTradeReason.dConflict)
            iL := -1

    // ---- 14. SIGNAL ---------------------------------------------------------
    // One position per direction. A second entry while one is still open is
    // refused here rather than left to SECTION 13, which cannot see this array.
    if iL >= 0
        if f_smHasActive(1)
            f_smKill(setups.get(iL), NoTradeReason.tradeOpen)
        else
            Setup s = setups.get(iL)
            f_smEnter(s, SetupState.active)
            longSignal := true
            sigIdxL    := iL
            sigPlanL   := s.plan
            sigScoreL  := s.score
            // Duplicate memory for SECTION 13, keyed on the frozen sweep
            // extreme - the identity of the liquidity event this trade is
            // built on, which is what "the same setup twice" actually means.
            array.set(dupLvl, f_slot(1), s.swpExt)
            array.set(dupBar, f_slot(1), bar_index)
    if iS >= 0
        if f_smHasActive(-1)
            f_smKill(setups.get(iS), NoTradeReason.tradeOpen)
        else
            Setup s2 = setups.get(iS)
            f_smEnter(s2, SetupState.active)
            shortSignal := true
            sigIdxS     := iS
            sigPlanS    := s2.plan
            sigScoreS   := s2.score
            array.set(dupLvl, f_slot(-1), s2.swpExt)
            array.set(dupBar, f_slot(-1), bar_index)

    // ---- 14b. REAP ----------------------------------------------------------
    // Anything still `armed` lost to a same-direction peer. dEvicted, not
    // dConflict: the cross-direction losers already died at step 13 and the two
    // causes must stay distinguishable in SECTION 15's histogram.
    f_smReapArmed(1,  NoTradeReason.dEvicted)
    f_smReapArmed(-1, NoTradeReason.dEvicted)

    // ---- 15. MANAGEMENT -----------------------------------------------------
    // `bar_index > s.stBar` is not a nicety. stBar is stamped when the setup
    // went active, i.e. at THIS bar's close, and the strategy twin fills at the
    // NEXT bar's open. Managing the signal bar's own range would book a stop or
    // a target from price action that happened BEFORE the signal existed, and
    // the indicator's ledger and the strategy's blotter would then disagree by
    // construction on exactly the impulse candles that matter.
    //
    // WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT: an `active` Setup is a SLOT and a drawing, not a
    // position. It is released on the ORIGINAL stop, on TP3, or on the time
    // stop. The POSITION - partials, the break-even move after TP1, the
    // R-multiple - is SECTION 15's VTrade and that is the authoritative book.
    // With break-even enabled the VTrade can therefore close a few bars before
    // its Setup does. That costs nothing: an active Setup does not consume a
    // candidate slot (see f_smLive), so a lingering one cannot starve the
    // engine, and the two are never summed.
    int nMan = setups.size()
    if nMan > 0
        for i = 0 to nMan - 1
            Setup s3 = setups.get(i)
            if s3.st == SetupState.active and bar_index > s3.stBar and not na(s3.plan)
                bool sHit = f_pathStop(s3.dir, s3.plan.stop)
                bool tHit = f_pathTgt(s3.dir, s3.plan.tp3)
                if sHit                       // path policy: the stop wins ties
                    f_smKill(s3, NoTradeReason.dStopped)
                else if tHit
                    f_smKill(s3, NoTradeReason.dTarget)
                else if bar_index - s3.stBar >= i_barsInTrade
                    f_smKill(s3, NoTradeReason.dTimeStop)

// MEMORY BOUND, since this array is the only unbounded-looking thing here.
// Within one bar: live <= 2 * i_maxSetupsPerDir (the dir-filtered cap), plus at
// most that many again killed on this bar and awaiting the next GC, plus two
// births. Every dead element is removed at the next bar's step 1, so
// setups.size() <= 4 * i_maxSetupsPerDir + 2 at every point in every bar, i.e.
// 10 at the default. Against a 500/500/500 drawing budget and 1 box + 1 label +
// 5 lines per setup that is a 7x margin on the tightest of the three.
// ========================= END SECTION 14 ===================================
